          "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"In the Jonah tradition, the great fish most commonly signifies the realm of death—the grave or Sheol—from which Jonah’s deliverance prefigures Christ’s burial and resurrection.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Jonah 1:17; 2:1–10 — Jonah descends into the fish, describes his plight in the language of Sheol and the pit, and is restored to life on land.\",\"Matthew 12:39–41 — Jesus explicitly identifies Jonah’s three days in the fish with the Son of Man’s three days in the heart of the earth.\",\"Matthew 16:4 and Luke 11:29–32 — the “sign of Jonah” is applied to Jesus, though Luke emphasizes Jonah’s prophetic role more than the fish itself.\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A sea-monster represents primordial chaos or hostile cosmic powers subdued by God.\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 74:13–14\",\"Isaiah 27:1\",\"Isaiah 51:9–10\",\"Job 7:12\",\"Genesis 1:21\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The fish is primarily a divinely appointed instrument of judgment, preservation, and providential rescue rather than a fixed symbol.\",\"basis\":[\"Jonah 1:17\",\"Jonah 2:10\",\"Jonah 4:6–8, where other created agents are likewise appointed by God\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A sea-monster symbolizes an oppressive earthly ruler or empire, especially Egypt and Pharaoh.\",\"basis\":[\"Ezekiel 29:3–5\",\"Ezekiel 32:2–8\",\"Isaiah 51:9–10\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"The great fish and Jonah’s emergence symbolize Christian baptism: descent into water and death followed by new life.\",\"basis\":[\"Jonah 1:17; 2:1–10\",\"Romans 6:3–4\",\"Early Christian typological use of Jonah in baptismal and funerary art\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"The sea-monster is identified directly with Satan or the apocalyptic dragon.\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 12:3–9\",\"Revelation 13:1–4\",\"Revelation 20:2\",\"Isaiah 27:1\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":false,\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"notes\":\"The death-and-resurrection meaning is dominant when “Great Fish” refers specifically to Jonah, because the New Testament supplies that typology. “Sea-monster” is broader, however, and commonly carries chaos, evil-power, or imperial imagery in other biblical contexts. Consequently, the combined term has no single settled symbolic meaning independent of context.\"}"
        }
      ],
